WEBVTT

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:01.880
table. But if you are a

00:00:01.759 --> 00:00:03.740
global enterprise, you are dealing

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:04.880
with thousands of concepts.

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:06.739
You can't just have all these

00:00:06.619 --> 00:00:08.299
entities thrown into one massive bucket,

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:09.100
it would be chaos.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:11.319
Total chaos. Which is why the

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.180
system relies heavily on namespaces

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:15.880
and inheritance. Namespaces act as

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:17.860
organizational boundaries or scopes

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:18.620
for your definitions.

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:21.460
So the HR department's definition of

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:23.880
a contract, which might mean an

00:00:23.759 --> 00:00:25.780
employment agreement, doesn't collide

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:27.440
and override the sales department's

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.220
definition of a contract, which means

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.560
a customer purchase agreement.

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.160
Exactly. Namespaces prevent those semantic

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:33.559
collisions,
